In article <474@nvuxk.UUCP> fleming@nvuxk.UUCP (George W. Fleming, fleming@nvuxk.cc.bellcore.com) writes:
>I have been trying to compile NH3 (patchlevel 3) on my PS/2 running OS2.
>I have MSC 5.1 and uses the Makefile.os2 that came with one of the patches.
>
>Even though I have everything that the Makefile says I need, I can't get
>it to compile...It gets through alloc.c without any problem, but when 
>it tries to compile makedefs.c, it bombs out with an "out of heap space"
>error.  I tried some of the "Dumb" options and they don't seem to help
>either. 

The message "out of heap space" means just that. The compiler is short of
memory while trying to compile. My guess is that for some reason you don't
have the full DOS memory available. Are you compiling in the "real" DOS
or the OS/2 compatibility box? If the latter, have you allocated enough
memory for the box? Note also that you need some 3 MB of free disk space.

I have compiled NH3 PL3 using makefile.os2 with all NH game options on and
without any auxiliary tricks both in PC-DOS 3.30 with 516 kB free memory
and in OS/2 1.1 EE DOS box with 433 kB free memory. I use NDMake 4.5 (which
is quite obvious by the makefile).

The game works fine with me except that it dies because of a random protection
fault at about 1% of the games. I haven't lost any really good games (read:
haven't had any) for that reason, but before I do and once I find the time, I
may be able to do a thing or two about that.

